comment {---- gui_main.d integration surface ----

`current()`/`load()`/`refreshCodeFromNode()` match `widget_panel.d`
(`WidgetPanelDialog`)'s own public API of the same names, so
gui_main.d's own call sites need only a mechanical swap, not a
rewrite. `onBeforeEdit`/`onEdited`/`onOpenExternalEditor` are the
same delegate-hook contract that class exposed too, assigned by
gui_main.d once, before `make_widget_panel()` runs (matching
`liveModeCb`/`overlayCb`/`okCb`'s own existing pre-construction
assignment requirement -- see those three's own comment above for
why: a `.callback(name)` assignment captures the delegate's *value*
at that statement, not a live reference to the variable).
`wireEditHooks()` is what actually threads `onBeforeEdit`/`onEdited`
through every property-editing widget under `widgetTabs` (including
both Grid sub-panels, reached transitively): gui_main.d calls it once
right after `make_widget_panel()` returns. Unlike the three .callback
hooks above, `onBeforeEdit`/`onEdited` are read from *inside* each
wrapped callback at press time, not captured at wrap time, so
`wireEditHooks()` itself has no ordering requirement relative to when
gui_main.d assigns them.

`onBeforeEdit`/`onEdited` fire around *every* callback under
`widgetTabs`, not just the ones `WidgetPanelDialog` used to gate
individually (that class called them from roughly 90 of its 129
callbacks, deliberately skipping e.g. the Browse buttons' own
cancelled-dialog path). This trades a small amount of over-firing
(a cancelled Browse dialog still pushes an undo checkpoint) for not
needing to hand-thread the pair into every callback in this file --
gui_main.d's own `checkpoint()` isn't a no-op on an unchanged
project, so this is a real, accepted imprecision, not a free
simplification; `onEdited`'s own redraw/recalcTree/redraw is
idempotent and costs nothing extra either way.}
  {
    in_source
    in_header
  }
